      "description": "A Carnegie Science-led team published findings in The Astrophysical Journal Letters using JWST's NIRSpec (Near-Infrared Spectrograph) instrument that provide the strongest evidence yet for an atmosphere on a rocky planet outside the Solar System. Measurements of the dayside temperature of TOI-561 b — an ultra-hot super-Earth orbiting its star in just 10.56 hours, one-fortieth the Mercury-Sun distance — showed approximately 1,800°C, dramatically cooler than the predicted 2,700°C for a bare rock. The temperature deficit of ~900°C can only be explained by a thick, volatile-rich atmosphere (water vapor, CO2, silicate clouds) redistributing heat to the permanently dark nightside, above a global magma ocean. TOI-561 b has 3.2 Earth masses, a radius 1.45 times Earth's, and orbits a star twice as old as the Sun in the Milky Way's 'thick disk.'",
      "context": "The detection of a persistent atmosphere on a world as extreme as TOI-561 b — which has endured billions of years of stellar radiation that should strip any gas layer — fundamentally challenges existing models of atmospheric retention and planetary evolution. The leading explanation is a dynamic magma ocean–atmosphere exchange: magma continuously outgasses volatiles that replenish the atmosphere as stellar radiation strips it away. If confirmed, this mechanism — a 'wet lava world' cycle — implies that volatile-rich rocky planets formed in metal-poor environments (like the Galactic thick disk) may commonly retain atmospheres, significantly broadening the range of planets potentially capable of sustaining complex chemistry or even habitable conditions at different epochs in cosmic history.",
      "timeline": [
        {"type": "background", "date": "2021-01-01", "label": "TOI-561 b identified; initial density measurements reveal the planet is less dense than expected for a purely rocky body, prompting atmospheric hypotheses."},
        {"type": "escalation", "date": "2024-05-01", "label": "JWST NIRSpec observes TOI-561 b system continuously for 37 hours during General Observers Program 3860, recording near-infrared emission spectrum during secondary eclipse."},
        {"type": "event", "date": "2025-12-11", "label": "Carnegie Science team publishes results in The Astrophysical Journal Letters: dayside temperature ~1,800°C vs. predicted 2,700°C confirms thick volatile-rich atmosphere above a global magma ocean."},
        {"type": "consequence", "date": "2026-03-22", "label": "ScienceDaily covers findings; NASA publishes official science release; team analyzes full 37-hour dataset to map temperature around the entire planet and narrow atmospheric composition."}
