        {"term": "NIRSpec (Near-Infrared Spectrograph)", "definition": "A JWST instrument used to measure the dayside temperature and emission spectrum of TOI-561 b during secondary eclipse — the key observational technique revealing the atmosphere."},
        {"term": "Magma Ocean Atmosphere Exchange", "definition": "The proposed mechanism sustaining TOI-561 b's atmosphere: volcanic outgassing from the global molten surface continuously replenishes gases stripped by stellar radiation."},
        {"term": "Ultra-Short Period Exoplanet", "definition": "A class of rocky planets with orbital periods under one Earth day, orbiting extremely close to their stars; TOI-561 b with a 10.56-hour period is among the most extreme examples."},
        {"term": "Galactic Thick Disk", "definition": "A structurally older, metal-poor region of the Milky Way where TOI-561 b's host star resides; planets formed there may have different chemical compositions and atmospheric evolution pathways."},
        {"term": "Secondary Eclipse", "definition": "The astronomical event when a planet passes behind its host star, allowing measurement of the planet's own emitted light; the technique used by JWST to measure TOI-561 b's dayside temperature."},
        {"term": "Volatile-Rich Atmosphere", "definition": "An atmosphere composed of easily-vaporized compounds including water vapor, carbon dioxide, and silicate gases; required to explain TOI-561 b's observed 900°C dayside temperature deficit."},
